# Synergistic Crystallization Kinetics Modulation and Deep/Shallow Level Defect Passivation via an Organometallic Cobaltocenium Salt Toward High‐Performance Inverted Perovskite Solar Cells

## abstract
Numerous deep/shallow level defects generated at the surface/grain boundaries of perovskite during uncontrollable crystallization pose a formidable challenge to the photovoltaic performance of perovskite solar cells (PSCs). Herein, an organometallic cobaltocenium salt additive, 1‐propanol‐2‐(1,2,3‐triazol‐4‐yl) cobaltocenium hexafluorophosphate (PTCoPF6), is incorporated into the perovskite precursor solution to regulate crystallization and minimize holistic defects for high‐performance inverted PSCs. The cobaltocenium cations and PF6− in PTCoPF6 stabilize the Pb‐I framework and repair the shallow‐level defects of positively and negatively charged vacancies in the perovskite. The N═N in the triazole ring of PTCoPF6 can passivate the deep‐level defects of uncoordinated lead. The interaction between PTCoPF6 and perovskite materials delays perovskite nucleation and crystal growth, ensuring high‐quality perovskite with large grains, and suppressing non‐radiative recombination and ion migration. Therefore, the PTCoPF6‐incorporated PSC achieves an impressive power conversion efficiency of 25.03% and outstanding long‐term stability. Unencapsulated and encapsulated PTCoPF6‐incorporated PSCs maintain 93% and 95% of their initial efficiencies under 85 °C storage in a nitrogen atmosphere for 1000 h and maximum power point tracking for nearly 1000 h, respectively. Synergistic crystallization kinetic modulation and deep/shallow level defect passivation with ionized metal‐organic complex additives will become prevalent methods to improve the efficiency and stability of PSCs.

Numerous deep/shallow level defects generated at the surface/grain boundaries of perovskite during uncontrollable crystallization pose a formidable challenge to the photovoltaic performance of perovskite solar cells (PSCs). Herein, an organometallic cobaltocenium salt additive, 1-propanol-2-(1,2,3-triazol-4-yl) cobaltocenium hexafluorophosphate $(P T C o P F_{6})$ , is incorporated into the perovskite precursor solution to regulate crystallization and minimize holistic defects for high-performance inverted PSCs. The cobaltocenium cations and $\mathsf{P F}_{6}^{-}$ in $\mathsf{P T C o P F}_{6}$ stabilize the Pb-I framework and repair the shallow-level defects of positively and negatively charged vacancies in the perovskite. The $\N=N$ in the triazole ring of ${\mathsf{P T C o P F}}_{6}$ can passivate the deep-level defects of uncoordinated lead. The interaction between ${\mathsf{P T C o P F}}_{6}$ and perovskite materials delays perovskite nucleation and crystal growth, ensuring high-quality perovskite with large grains, and suppressing non-radiative recombination and ion migration. Therefore, the ${\mathsf{P T C o P F}}_{6}$ -incorporated PSC achieves an impressive power conversion efficiency of $25.03\%$ and outstanding long-term stability. Unencapsulated and encapsulated ${\mathsf{P T C o P F}}_{6}$ -incorporated PSCs maintain $93\%$ and $95\%$ of their initial efficiencies under $85^{\circ}C$ storage in a nitrogen atmosphere for $\boldsymbol{\mathsf{1000}}\boldsymbol{\mathsf{h}}$ and maximum power point tracking for nearly $\mathsf{1000h}$ , respectively. Synergistic crystallization kinetic modulation and deep/shallow level defect passivation with ionized metal-organic complex additives will become prevalent methods to improve the efficiency and stability of PSCs.
## 1. Introduction
The inverted (p-i-n) perovskite solar cells (PSCs) have attained widespread attention due to their compatibility with different bottom cells for tandem photovoltaics,[1,2] cost-effectiveness,[3,4] low-temperature processability,[5–7] and accredited power conversion efficiency (PCE) of $26.1\%$ ,[8–10] which approaches the PCE of the most advanced crystalline silicon solar cells.[11] However, unavoidably abundant deep-level defects (dominated by uncoordinated $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}/\mathrm{I}^{-}$ and $\mathrm{Pb^{0}}$ clusters) and shallow-level defects (dominated by organic cations and halogen vacancies) are created during perovskite crystallization and practical running owing to the ionic crystal properties of perovskite.[12–15] These defects present the primary challenges in further improving the efficiency and stability of inverted PSCs.
To date, additive engineering has been widely demonstrated as an efficient strategy for managing defects in perovskite crystals.[16–18] Scholars have proposed various additives, such as organic salts,[19,20] small molecules,[21,22] polymers,[23,24] and low-dimensional perovskite[25,26] to achieve both crystallization regulation and defect passivation in perovskite films. Among them, organic salts stand out due to their abundant functional groups and structural diversity.[27,28] For instance, Meng et al.[29] introduced isobutylammonium isobutyldithiocarbamate (iBA-iBDTC) as an additive to effectively passivate defects and stabilize the structure of perovskite, thus realizing a high PCE of up to $24.25\%$ . Wu et al.[30] employed a dual-functional additive, fluoro- $\mathbf{N},\mathbf{N},\mathbf{N}^{\prime\prime},\mathbf{N}^{\prime\prime}$ -tetramethylformamidinium hexafluorophosphate (TFFH), to optimize crystallization dynamics, realizing in situ defect passivation and achieving an efficiency of $24.62\%$ . Recently, an organometallic compound, ferrocenylbis-thiophene-2-carboxylate $\left(\mathrm{FcTc}_{2}\right)$ , was applied to perovskite interface functionalization to reduce surface defects and facilitate interfacial carrier transfer through the electron-rich and electron-delocalizable ferrocene units.[31] However, there are few studies investigating compounds as additives that have both the defect passivation ability of organic salts and the outstanding charge transport capabilities of metal-organic ligands to tackle perovskite non-radiative recombination and charge transport problems.
Herein, we synthesize the highly conductive 1-propanol2-(1,2,3-triazol-4-yl) cobaltocenium hexafluorophosphate $(\mathrm{PTCoPF}_{6}$ , Figure 1a) as a multifunctional additive to control the perovskite crystallization and passivate holistic defects for fabricating high-quality perovskite films synergistically. The cobaltocenium cations and $\mathrm{PF}_{6}^{\mathrm{~-~}}$ in ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ play a pivotal role in stabilizing the Pb-I framework and repairing the shallowlevel defects of positively and negatively charged vacancies in the perovskite. Furthermore, the ${\mathrm{N}}{=}{\mathrm{N}}$ in the triazole ring of ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ can effectively passivate the deep-level defects of uncoordinated lead (Figure 1b). The interaction between ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ and perovskite materials serves to retard perovskite nucleation and crystal growth, ensuring the formation of high-quality perovskite, and suppressing non-radiative recombination and ion migration. Consequently, this synergistic strategy contributes to increasing the PCE of inverted PSC from $23.25\%$ (control PSC) to $25.03\%$ $(\mathrm{PTCoPF}_{6}$ -incorporated PSC). Notably, the thermal stability of the unencapsulated ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ -incorporated PSC is significantly improved, maintaining $93\%$ of its original efficiency after heating at $85~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $1000\mathrm{h}$ in a nitrogen $(\Nu_{2})$ atmosphere. In addition, under maximum power point tracking monitoring, the ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ -incorporated PSC retains its initial efficiency of $95\%$ after sustained running for $1000\mathrm{h}$ .
## 2. Results and Discussion
The synthesis process of ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ was described in the Experimental section of the Supporting Information. The designed molecular structure of ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ was confirmed by 1H nuclear magnetic resonance (1H-NMR, Figure S1, Supporting Information) and Fourier transform infrared (FT-IR, Figure S2, Supporting Information).[32,33] We calculated the electrostatic potential (ESP) using density functional theory (DFT) to analyze the electron density distribution of ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ (Figure 1c). The high electron density (blue region) is mainly distributed on the triazole ring and $\mathrm{PF}_{6}^{\phantom{-}-}$ , giving favorable conditions for coordination with uncoordinated $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ . The low electron density (red region) is mainly concentrated on the cobaltocenium and −OH group, providing beneficial conditions for compensating cation vacancies in the perovskite. In addition, we also calculate the ESP of 1-propanol-2-(1,2,3-triazol-4-yl) ferrocene hexafluorophosphate $({\mathrm{PTFePF}}_{6})$ to compare the effects of different metallocene units on electron density distribution (Figure S3, Supporting Information). Compared with ${\mathrm{PTFePF}}_{6}$ , ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ exhibits a lower electron density close to metallocene and a higher electron density close to $\mathrm{PF}_{6}^{-}$ , indicating that ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ has a stronger ability to passivate defects than ${\mathrm{PTFePF}}_{6}$ . Based on the structural analysis and theoretical calculation, we speculate that the ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ with multiple defect passivation sites can simultaneously achieve deep-level defect passivation and shallow-level vacancy defect compensating in the perovskite film.
To validate the above conjectures, we initially explored the chemical interaction between ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ and perovskite by performing FT-IR characterization on the pure ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ , ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ - $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ , pure FAI (formamidine iodide), and ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ -FAI samples. In the pure ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ spectrum, the peaks located at 1416.1 and $818.7~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are ascribed to the ${\mathrm{N}}{=}{\mathrm{N}}$ stretching vibration of the triazole ring and the P─F stretching vibration of $\mathrm{PF}_{6}^{-}$ , respectively. After mixing with $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ , the peaks of ${\mathrm{N}}{=}{\mathrm{N}}$ and $\mathrm{{P-F}}$ stretching vibrations are respectively shifted to a lower wavenumber of $1413.4~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and a higher wavenumber of $829.5~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (Figure 1d,e). These results imply that the $_{\mathrm{N=N}}$ of the triazole ring and $\mathrm{PF}_{6}^{\phantom{-}-}$ in the ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ , possibly as a Lewis base, interact with the uncoordinated $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ .[34,35] In addition, when FAI is mixed with ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ , the $\mathrm{C}{=}\mathrm{N}$ stretching vibration in FAI is shifted from 1692.2 to $1695.3\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , and the P─F stretching vibration in ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ is shifted from 818.7 to $827.8\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , possibly owing to the formation of an ionic bond between $\mathrm{PF}_{6}^{\phantom{-}-}$ and $\mathrm{FA^{+}}$ .[36] We further searched for evidence of the potential chemical interaction between ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ and CsFAMA-based perovskite (MA: methylammonium) via the high-resolution X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) measurement. The Pb 4f peaks in the perovskite are shifted toward a lower binding energy by $0.15\mathrm{~eV}$ after incorporating ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ , indicating the strong interaction between ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ and uncoordinated $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ (Figure 1h). Notably, two additional peaks at 136.5 and $141.4\ \mathrm{eV}$ associated with the metallic ${\mathrm{Pb}}^{0}$ are found in the control film, which are by-products of the decomposition of residual $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ on the surface under Xray irradiation.[37] Inspiringly, the metallic ${\mathrm{Pb}}^{0}$ is significantly reduced in the ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ -incorporated film, which indicates that ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ can reduce residual $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ on the surface, thereby reducing the appearance of metal ${\mathrm{Pb}}^{0}$ . Moreover, the I 3d peaks also move to lower binding energies in ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ -incorporated perovskite film, which may indicate that $\mathrm{PF}_{6}^{\phantom{-}-}$ in ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ compensates for halogen anion vacancy defects by generating electrostatic interaction with positively charged vacancies (Figure S4, Supporting Information).[38]
DFT calculations were also carried out to support the interaction between ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ and perovskite via the Vienna Ab initio Simulation Package code. We first chose the $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ -terminated perovskite surface as a model, as it has been proven to be stable and possesses the lowest energy configuration.[31] The calculation results reveal that the binding energy of the Pb─N bond between the triazole ring from ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ and uncoordinated $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ from perovskite is $-1.29\ \mathrm{eV},$ which confirms the strong coordination ability of ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ , thus achieving the passivation of uncoordinated $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ defects (Figure 1i). The binding energy between $\mathrm{PF}_{6}^{\phantom{-}-}$ and the I vacancy is $-1.64\ \mathrm{eV},$ which demonstrates that $\mathrm{PF_{6}}^{-}$ can take up the I vacancy (Figure 1j). Additionally, the FAIterminated perovskite surface is adopted to evaluate the impact of the ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ on the FA vacancy defect. The binding energy between cobaltocenium cations from ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ and FA vacancy from perovskite is estimated to be $-1.66\ \mathrm{eV},$ indicating that the cobaltocenium cations can fill in the FA vacancy (Figure 1k). The binding energies of the $-\mathrm{OH}$ group in ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ filling the I vacancy and the FA vacancy in the perovskite film are $+0.77$ and $+0.75\ \mathrm{eV},$ respectively, implying that the $-\mathrm{OH}$ group is not conducive to filling the vacancy defects of perovskite (Figure S5, Supporting Information). Hence, the ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ can act by passivating both the deep and shallow-level defects via coordination passivation and vacancy healing, which is also beneficial for stabilizing the components in perovskite.
We explored the impact of the strong interaction between ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ and perovskite on the quality of perovskite films. We first compare the differences in the morphology of the perovskite films before and after ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ incorporation via scanning electron microscopy (SEM, Figure S6, Supporting Information). Notably, the white phase confirmed as $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ according to previous reports[39,40] is appropriately reduced in ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ - incorporated perovskite film, which may be helpful in improving the stability of perovskite (Figure 2a,b). Meanwhile, the ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ -incorporated perovskite film exhibits an increased average grain size (D) from 271 to $342\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 2c,d). In addition, the ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ -incorporated perovskite film exhibits a similar thickness of ${\approx}700\ \mathrm{nm}$ but more orderly grain distribution and better vertical growth than the control film, which is conducive to charge transfer (Figure 2e,f).[41] Atomic force microscope (AFM) images show the reduced average roughness for ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ -incorporated perovskite film, which is beneficial for contact with the top layer (Figure S7, Supporting Information).[42] A narrowed peak in the (100) plane of X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns proves an improved crystallinity in ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ -incorporated perovskite film (Figure S8, Supporting Information), which is also confirmed in UV–vis absorption spectra (Figure S9, Supporting Information). Meanwhile, a blue-shifted (100) peak position is also observed in ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ -incorporated perovskite film, indicating crystal expansion. Because the radius of $\mathrm{PF}_{6}{}^{-}$ $255\mathrm{pm})$ is larger than the radius of $\mathrm{~I^{-}~}$ $(220\mathrm{pm})$ , $\mathrm{PF}_{6}^{\phantom{-}-}$ in ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ occupies the I vacancy and causes the perovskite lattice to expand.[43]
We focused on investigating the influence of ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ on both the electrical conductivity and distribution characteristics within perovskite films. The electronic properties of control and ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ -incorporated perovskite films were measured via the conducting AFM (c-AFM) (Figure 2g,h). The corresponding local dark conductivity difference is shown in Figure 2i. A significant increment in current is observed, especially in the grain boundaries region for ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ -incorporated perovskite films, which indicates that the incorporation of ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ can improve the conductivity of perovskite film, possibly ascribed to the distribution of ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ with excellent conductivity at grain boundaries and the improved crystal quality of perovskite film.[44] Timeof-flight secondary ion mass spectrometry (TOF-SIMS) results demonstrate that the characteristic elements (P, F) of ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ are mainly distributed on the bulk of perovskite film, which is favorable for omnidirectional defect passivation of ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ in the perovskite grain boundary region (Figure S10, Supporting Information).
The in situ UV–vis absorption was used to monitor the perovskite formation process during spin-coating in order to further study the influence of ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ on perovskite crystallization (Figure S11, Supporting Information).[45] The 2D contour plots of in situ UV–vis absorption spectra are presented in Figure 3a,b.
In the first $38~\mathrm{s}$ , the absorption below $450\mathrm{nm}$ is ascribed to the precursor solution, which shows negligible nucleation of perovskite. After the drop of antisolvent at $38\mathrm{~s~}$ , the absorption intensity in the range of $400{\-}900\ \mathrm{nm}$ begins to increase, indicating the formation of perovskite. To plainly compare the difference in control and ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ -incorporated perovskite evolution, we extract the absorption intensity at $600\ \mathrm{nm}$ as a function of spin-coating time (Figure 3c). Subsequently, we calculate the nucleation rate of perovskite during the nucleation stage according to the first derivative (Figure 3d).[46] Notably, the nucleation rate of perovskite is slowed down after the incorporation of ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ , suggesting that ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ can cause delayed nucleation kinetics.[47] Owing to the specific interaction of ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ and perovskite, extra energy is required to overcome these forces to form the perovskite, thus leading to sluggish nucleation and crystal growth. This is beneficial for the ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ -incorporated film in achieving high-quality perovskite films with large grain sizes and low redundant $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ , which is also proved by SEM and XRD results. In view of the above analysis, we can infer the likely role of ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ in the crystallization processes of perovskite and schematically plot it in Figure 3e. Due to the rapid evaporation of solvents during spin-coating and thermal annealing procedures, perovskite tends to undergo rapid nucleation and growth, easily leading to small grains and a certain number of structural defects (i.e., uncoordinated $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ and FA/I vacancy), which results in the poor photovoltaic performance of perovskite film. Nevertheless, due to the additional energy needed to overcome the strong $\mathrm{FA}{\cdots}\mathrm{PTCoPF}_{6}{\cdots}\mathrm{Pb}{\-}\mathrm{I}$ interaction for the perovskite formation, the entire perovskite nucleation and growth are efficiently retarded during crystallization, which can enlarge the grain size and enhance the crystallinity of the perovskite film. More importantly, deep- and shallowlevel defects are also in situ suppressed and passivated during the perovskite formation. In general, the ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ realizes precise perovskite crystallization regulation and holistic defect elimination.
Profiting from the enhancement of perovskite quality produced by ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ incorporation, we fabricated the inverted PSC with an architecture of fluorine-doped tin oxide (FTO) /nickel oxide (NiOx) /poly(triarylamine) (PTAA) /aluminum trioxide $(\mathsf{A l}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}$ ) /CsFAMA-based perovskite /6, 6-phenyl $\mathrm{C}_{61}$ -butyric acid methyl ester $(\mathrm{PC}_{61}\mathrm{BM})+$ fullerene $\ensuremath{\left(\mathrm{C}_{60}\right)}$ /bathocuproine (BCP) $/\mathrm{Ag}$ (Figure 4a). Figure S12 (Supporting Information) and Figure 4b show the statistical distribution and best $J{-}V$ (current density–voltage) curves of PSCs with different concentrations of ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ , and corresponding photovoltaic parameters containing open-circuit voltage $(V_{\mathrm{OC}})$ , short-circuit current density $(J_{\scriptscriptstyle\mathrm{SC}})$ , fill factor (FF), and PCE are collected in Table S1 (Supporting Information). The control PSC delivers an average PCE of $22.39\pm0.41\%$ and a champion PCE of $23.25\%$ , with a $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ of $1.140~\mathrm{V}_{:}$ a $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ of $25.45\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ , and an FF of $80.12\%$ in the reverse scan. After incorporating ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ , the performances of PSCs are all improved, especially for the $1\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ -incorporated PSC, which yields an average PCE of $24.38\pm0.35\%$ and a best PCE of $25.03\%$ , a $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ of $1.164~\mathrm{V},$ a $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ of $25.65~\mathrm{mA~cm}^{-2}$ , and an FF of $83.83\%$ in the reverse scan. Meanwhile, the $1\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ PTCoPF6-incorporated PSCs exhibit a modest standard deviation from the average PCE, revealing great repeatability. Therefore, the optimal concentration of ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ is determined to be $1\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ . Subsequently, we measure the $J{\cdot}V$ curves of the best-performing PSCs without and with ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ incorporation under forward and reverse scans to evaluate the hysteresis behaviors (Figure 4c,d). The hysteresis factor (HF) can be calculated from the equation for $\begin{array}{r}{|\dot{H}\dot{F}\ =\frac{P C E_{\mathrm{reverse}}-P C E_{\mathrm{forward}}}{P C E_{\mathrm{reverse}}}\dot{}}\end{array}$ ).[48] The ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ -incorporated PSC exhibits a smaller HF of $1.12\%$ compared with the control PSC $(4.60\%)$ , suggesting suppressed defects in the ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ -incorporated PSC. The steady power output (SPO) and steady-state photocurrent of PSCs are monitored under their maximum power point (MPP) voltages for $300\mathrm{~s~}$ (Figure 4e). The photocurrent of ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ -incorporated PSC stabilizes at $24.31\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , which corresponds to a stable SPO of $24.80\%$ . On the contrary, the control PSC shows an attenuation trend in output performance during the illumination, finally delivering a photocurrent of $23.59\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ corresponding to an SPO of $22.36\%$ . From the typical external quantum efficiency (EQE) spectra, the integrated current densities are derived to be
24.46 and $24.67\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ for control and ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ -incorporated PSCs, separately, which are comparable to the corresponding $J{-}V$ results (Figure 4f). The advantage of cobaltocenium-based ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ over ferrocene-based ${\mathrm{PTFePF}}_{6}$ in PCE is further verified in Figure S13 (Supporting Information).
To understand the kinetics of charge carriers in perovskite films with various concentrations of ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ , we conducted steady-state photoluminescence (PL) and time-resolved photoluminescence (TRPL) characterizations. An obvious increase in PL intensity and blueshift is found for perovskite film after the ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ incorporation (Figure 5a). Moreover, the PL mapping also demonstrates that the ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ -incorporated perovskite film exhibits a higher PL intensity with more uniform distribution over a large scale in comparison with the control one, which is an indication of low defect density and reduced nonradiative recombination (Figure 5b,c). Subsequently, the TRPL curves of different perovskite films are fitted by a biexponential equation, and corresponding data are recorded in Table S2 (Supporting Information). In comparison with the control perovskite film with a short carrier average lifetime $(\tau_{\mathrm{ave}})$ of $1.28~\upmu\mathrm{s}$ , the ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ - incorporated perovskite film presents an extended $\tau_{\mathrm{ave}}$ over 1.5 times to $2.22~\upmu\mathrm{s}$ , further confirming the superior passivation effect of ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ (Figure 5d). Furthermore, the $\tau_{\mathrm{ave}}$ mapping images also show that the $\tau_{\mathrm{ave}}$ in the ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ -incorporated perovskite film is significantly prolonged (Figure 5e,f). These results reveal that there is low defect density and reduced charge recombination in ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ -incorporated perovskite film, which is consistent with improved photovoltaic performance.
The surface energetics of the perovskite films were characterized to elucidate the reason for the increase in $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ value via Ultraviolet photoelectron spectroscopy (UPS, Figure $\mathrm{5g})$ . The energy bands of control and ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ -incorporated perovskite films are calculated from UPS results and the bandgaps $(E_{\mathrm{g}})$ of perovskite (Figure S14, Supporting Information), as schematically illustrated in Figure 5h. When the ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ is incorporated into the perovskite, a shifted Fermi level $\left(E_{\mathrm{F}}\right)$ from $-4.48$ to $-4.32\ \mathrm{eV}$ is observed, closer to the conduction band (CB), revealing a more n-type nature of the ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ -incorporated perovskite. This n-type characteristic film matches better with each functional layer, which helps with $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ increment and charge extraction. We then carried out the Mott-Schottky measurement to analyze the built-in potential $(V_{\mathrm{bi}})$ in the PSCs. As shown in Figure 5i, the $V_{\mathrm{bi}}$ value of the ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ -incorporated PSC is evaluated to be $1.13~\mathrm{V},$ which is higher than that of the control one (1.09 V), possibly due to the shifted-up $E_{\mathrm{{F}}}$ . The increased $V_{\mathrm{bi}}$ favors charge carrier separation in ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ -incorporated PSC, which is directly related to the improved $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ . Subsequently, we measured the defect density via admittance spectroscopy combined with Mott-Schottky analysis, which could characterize the trap density of states (tDOS) distribution within the perovskite film. As specifically shown in Figure 5j, the energy range of $0.35-$ $0.40\mathrm{eV}$ is ascribed to the shallow-level trap region, and the energy range of $0.40{\-}0.60\mathrm{eV}$ corresponds to the deep-level trap region. A lower trap density is observed in the ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ -incorporated film compared with the control one over the whole trap depth, especially in the shallow-level trap region, implying that simultaneous inhibition of deep/shallow-level defects in perovskite film can be achieved via the ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ incorporation.
We measured the relationship between the $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ values and light intensities to gain insight into understanding the mechanism of charge recombination in the entire device (Figure 5k). Generally, the ideality factor $(n)$ is assessed via linearly fitting the $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ values as the function of light intensity following Equation (1):[49]
$$
V_{\mathrm{OC}}=\frac{\mathrm{n}k_{\mathrm{B}}T}{q}l n\left(I\right)+c o n s t a n t
$$
Where $k_{\mathrm{B}}$ represents the Boltzmann constant, $T$ represents the thermodynamic temperature, $q$ represents the electron charge, and $I$ is the light intensity. The control and ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ - incorporated PSC exhibit $n$ values of 1.56 and 1.21, respectively. The $n$ value of the ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ -incorporated PSC is closer to the ideal value of 1 for the trap-free diode, suggesting suppressed non-radiative carrier recombination within the ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ -incorporated PSC, which explains the reason for the increase in FF.[50] Subsequently, the same conclusion was reached using electrochemical impedance spectroscopy measurements (Figure 5l). We fit the Nyquist plots of the devices through an equivalent circuit model and summarize the corresponding parameters in Table S3 (Supporting Information). A higher recombination resistance is found for ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ -incorporated PSC than that for control PSC, thus demonstrating the suppressed charge recombination in the ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ -incorporated perovskite film.[51] In addition, the reduced defect density and accelerated carrier mobility in the ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ -incorporated device are verified using the space charge limited current method (Figure S15, Supporting Information). This can be attributed to the effective defect suppression, optimized energy band structure, and improved conductivity of perovskite film.
Long-term stability was an important factor related to the practical application of perovskite photovoltaics.[52] Therefore, we assessed the effect of ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ incorporation on the stability of PSC under different conditions. When the unencapsulated PSCs are aged at $85~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in an $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ atmosphere for $1000\mathrm{~h~}$ , the ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ - incorporated PSCs exhibit excellent stability, with PCE retention values of $93\%$ of their initial PCEs (Figure 6a). However, the PCEs are degraded to $56\%$ of their original values for the control PSCs under the same conditions. We also monitored the changes in XRD patterns for the perovskite films after the thermal aging at $85~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $300\mathrm{~h~}$ in the $\Nu_{2}$ atmosphere. The control perovskite film is decomposed into more $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ in comparison with the ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ -incorporated film (Figure 6b,c). The chemically reactive components, such as $\mathrm{FA^{+}}$ and $\mathrm{MA^{+}}$ , in the perovskite can easily volatilize under thermal pressure, resulting in the formation of $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ .[53] ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ stabilizes the perovskite lattices and reduces the volatilization of organic cations, which thus enhances the thermal stability of perovskite. Next, we tested the operational stability of PSCs by tracking the encapsulated PSC at the MPP under constant one-sun illumination. In Figure 6d, the ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ -incorporated PSC keeps an initial efficiency of $95\%$ after tracking for $1000\mathrm{~h~}$ , showing outstanding stability. In addition, a $T_{\mathfrak{s o}}$ (time to $90\%$ original PCE) of $3080\mathrm{~h~}$ is estimated for the ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ -incorporated PSC according to the method reported by Khenkin et al.[54] In sharp contrast, the performance of the control decays rapidly, sustaining $\approx76\%$ of its original efficiency after $600\mathrm{h}$ , and its $T_{\mathfrak{s o}}$ is measured to be only $230\mathrm{h}$ . To analyze the reasons for the improved operational stability of PSCs, we implemented the TOF-SIMS for the PSCs before and under full-spectrum sunlight in an $\Nu_{2}$ environment for $500\mathrm{{h}}$ . As shown in Figure $_{\mathrm{6e,f}}$ the outward diffusion of I− and $\mathsf{P b I^{-}}$ ions from the perovskite is more obvious than that in ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ -incorporated PSC after light irradiation. We conclude that ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ prevents ion migration through vacancy healing and coordination passivation, thereby suppressing the decomposition of the perovskite material and resulting in improved operational stability of PSCs. Taken together, these results show that the concurrent passivation of deep- and shallow-level defects plays a vital influence in obtaining high-stability PSCs.
## 3. Conclusion
In summary, we have successfully obtained high-quality and stable perovskite film through a highly conductive ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ additive. The cobaltocenium cations and $\mathrm{PF}_{6}^{\phantom{-}-}$ in ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ stabilize the Pb-I framework and heal the shallow-level defects of vacancies in the perovskite. Meanwhile, the $\mathrm{{N=N}}$ of the triazole ring in ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ effectively passivates the deep-level defects of uncoordinated lead. Moreover, ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ strongly interacts with perovskite materials to controllably modulate the perovskite crystallization, which ensures high-quality perovskite and inhibits non-radiative recombination and ion migration. Consequently, the ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ -incorporated PSC achieves a high efficiency of $25.03\%$ and much improved long-term stability. The unencapsulated ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ -incorporated PSC retains $93\%$ of its original efficiency after $1000\mathrm{h}$ of storage at $85~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in a nitrogen atmosphere. The encapsulated ${\mathrm{PTCoPF}}_{6}$ -incorporated PSC sustains $95\%$ of its initial efficiency under MPP tracking for nearly $1000\mathrm{h}$ . This work identifies a successful strategy for enhancing the performance of PSCs with highly efficient additives.
